      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2012 European Federation of International Association for the Study of Pain Chapters.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Lacosamide is a novel anti-epileptic drug that enhances the slow- and not fast-inactivating state of voltage-gated sodium channels. Lacosamide has demonstrated analgesic efficacy in several animal studies but preclinical studies on neuropathic pain models are rare, and recent clinical trials showed no superior analgesic effects."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Here, we examine whether an acute or chronic administration of lacosamide (3-60\u2009mg/kg, i.p.) attenuates pain behaviour induced by spinal nerve ligation (SNL). To validate the inhibitory efficacy of lacosamide on voltage-gated sodium channels, sodium currents in naïve and SNL-injured dorsal root ganglion (DRG) neurons were recorded using whole-cell patch clamping."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Lacosamide only marginally attenuated thermal hyperalgesia, but not tactile allodynia when applied once 7 or 14 days after SNL and showed no analgesic effect when applied daily for 19 days. In naïve neurons, 100\u2009μmol/L lacosamide inhibited sodium channel currents by 58% and enhanced the slow inactivation (87% for lacosamide vs. 47% for control). In contrast, lacosamide inhibited sodium currents in injured DRG neurons by only 15%, while the effects on slow inactivation were diminished. Isolated currents from the NaV 1.8 channel subtype were only marginally changed by lacosamide."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The reduced effectiveness of lacosamide on voltage-gated sodium channel currents in injured DRG neurons may contribute to the reduced analgesic effect observed for the SNL model."
          }
        ]
      },
